building something internal and don't want to maintain yet another list of who's allowed in? don't. the directory can gate your login on an entitlement, tell your app which entitlements someone has, or both. you don't write any directory code. it all comes through the normal [OIDC](<%= doc_path("oidc-guide") %>) / [OAuth](<%= doc_path("oauth-guide") %>) flow you're already doing.

that means no invite flow and no user admin page. create the account the first time someone signs in, give them whatever role their groups claim says, and let the team that owns them handle who's in and who's out. when someone loses access they can't sign in again. if your app keeps its own sessions (it probably does), read the bit about sessions below, or they'll stay signed in until that session runs out.

your app has to be `hq_official` for any of this. community apps can't be wired up.

### getting set up

first figure out what access your app actually needs. name it after what the app does, not after a team: `event-staff` for the check-in app, `bank-admins` for the admin side of bank, that kind of thing. most apps need one or two. you can't hook up a group directly. groups are the org chart and they change constantly, and you don't want a reorg deciding who gets into your app.

then file an infra ticket under "Hack Club Auth (internal)" asking for the entitlement. say who should be in it, and whether you want to wire it up yourself (you'll get `expose` on it) or want someone to do it for you.

if you want the claim, your app also needs the `groups` scope. whoever made your app official, or a program admin, can add it.

### who can wire things up

directory admins can wire any official app from [directory apps](<%= directory_apps_path %>). app owners and collaborators can wire the entitlements they have `expose` on, from their app's page under the **Groups & Access** tab in [developer apps](<%= developer_apps_path %>).

### only letting certain people in

add one or more entitlements as requirements. being in any one of them is enough to sign in.

anyone who doesn't qualify gets sent back to your redirect URI with `error=access_denied` and your `state`, same as if they'd hit cancel. that works for `prompt=none` and `response_mode=form_post` too. if the redirect URI isn't one you registered, they end up on one of our pages instead.

we only check when someone signs in. if they lose the entitlement later (someone removes them, their team gets removed, it expires), we revoke their tokens for your app. that only does something if your app uses those tokens again, by refreshing or calling userinfo. most apps don't, so if you actually need people kicked out, keep your sessions short, set up a session revoker, or refresh every so often.

careful when you remove requirements. removing one logs out anyone who only got in through it. removing the last one opens your app to everyone with a hack club auth account. the confirm box will yell at you about that.

### knowing who's who

ask for the `groups` scope and add your entitlements on the **Groups & Access** tab. the claim name defaults to the entitlement name; use a custom one if you need to. the id token and userinfo then include:

```json
{ "groups": ["staff", "design"] }
```

that's only the names you set. the id token's copy is whatever was true when they signed in and never changes. userinfo works it out fresh on every call, so if your app makes decisions off it, call userinfo when it matters instead of trusting the id token from last week.

### if your app has its own sessions

if you only take one thing from this page: our tokens don't log anyone out of your app. your session does. keep it short, or have a directory admin set up a session revoker for your app on [directory apps](<%= directory_apps_path %>), and we'll tell you when someone loses access.

with a webhook, we `POST` `{"email": "person@example.com"}` to your https URL with `Authorization: Bearer <your secret>`. kill their sessions and send back a 2xx. if you error we'll keep retrying for a few hours. the URL has to be public, and changing it means re-entering the secret.

with cloudflare access we call cloudflare's revoke for that email. cloudflare can only do that for the whole account, so they get signed out of every access app you have, not just yours. to make that painless, have your access policy check our `groups` claim, and turn on instant auth with hack club auth as the only login option. then all the other apps sign them straight back in, and only yours says no.

### SAML

SAML apps live in `config/saml.yml`, so changes go through a PR. add `entitlements: { staff: "Staff" }` and the app gets a `groups` attribute with those values (rename it with `groups_attribute`). the SP has to actually request that attribute, or we'll refuse to boot. people with nothing get an empty one, so apps that sync groups clean up after themselves. `required_entitlements: [staff]` gates sign-in the same way requirements do.

SAML has no tokens to revoke, so losing an entitlement only stops the next sign-in. an existing session keeps working until it runs out. and you can't delete or rename an entitlement while `saml.yml` still uses it.
